stream
BT /F1 9 Tf 11 TL 54 740 Td
(# 10 Recommended operating conditions) '
() '
(Table 10-1. Recommended operating conditions.) '
() '
(  Parameter                          Min     Typ     Max     Unit) '
(  Supply voltage VDD                 3.0     3.3     3.6     V) '
(  Supply current, active                     6.5     9.0     mA) '
(  Supply current, low-power mode             1.2     1.8     mA) '
(  Operating ambient temperature      -40             125     \260C) '
(  Magnetic flux density              30      60      90      mT) '
(  Air gap \(6 mm x 2.5 mm magnet\)     0.5     1.5     3.0     mm) '
(  Rotational speed                           0       30000   rpm) '
(  I\262C clock frequency                0               1000    kHz) '
(  Supply ripple, 1 kHz to 1 MHz                      50      mV pp) '
() '
(## 10.1 Supply decoupling) '
() '
(Place a 100 nF ceramic capacitor within 2 mm of the VDD pin and a 4.7 \265F) '
(capacitor within 10 mm. The device draws current pulses at the 8 kHz) '
(sampling rate. Without the 4.7 \265F capacitor these pulses can modulate the) '
(supply enough to add 1-2 LSB of noise.) '
() '
(## 10.2 Shared supplies) '
() '
(When the encoder shares a 3.3 V rail with a motor driver or a radio,) '
(consider a separate LDO or an RC filter \(10 Ohm, 10 \265F\) on VDD. Supply dips) '
(below VBOR = 2.6 V cause a brown-out reset \(page 11\). The brown-out reset) '
(clears CONF2 \(page 58\).) '
() '
(## 10.3 Rotational speed) '
() '
(The angle output is valid up to 30,000 rpm. At high speed the output lags) '
(the true angle by the internal processing delay plus the FILT time constant) '
(\(page 12 and page 31\). The lag is a pure function of speed and can be) '
(compensated by the host if needed.) '
ET
BT /F1 7.5 Tf 200.25 36 Td (ENCX14-RM rev 1.2    CC-BY-4.0    page 10 of 60) Tj ET
endstream
endobj
25 0 obj
<< /Type /Page /Parent 2 0 R /MediaBox [0 0 612 792] /Resources << /Font << /F1 3 0 R >> >> /Contents 26 0 R >>
endobj
26 0 obj
<< /Length 2107 >>
stream
BT /F1 9 Tf 11 TL 54 740 Td
(# 11 Supply, power-on reset and brown-out reset) '
() '
(## 11.1 Reset sources) '
() '
(The device has four reset sources. All four drive the same internal reset) '
(line and have the same effect on the registers.) '
() '
(  Source        Trigger                                     Page) '
(  Power-on      VDD rises through VPOR = 2.8 V              11) '
(  Brown-out     VDD falls below VBOR = 2.6 V for > 2 \265s     11) '
(  Watchdog      internal oscillator stalls for > 1 ms       54) '
(  Soft reset    host writes 0xA5 to CMD \(0x30\)              36) '
() '
(## 11.2 Effect of a reset) '
() '
(On every reset:) '
() '
(  - CONF2 returns to 0x00. FILT = 0 \(filter off\), DIR = 0, HYST = 0, WD = 0.) '
(  - STATUS is cleared and then STATUS.RST \(bit 0\) is set.) '
(  - ZPOS, MPOS and CONF1 are reloaded from OTP.) '
(  - The I\262C interface is idle and ready 1.5 ms after the reset ends.) '
(  - The first valid angle is available 2.0 ms after the reset ends.) '
() '
(## 11.3 Thresholds) '
() '
(Table 11-1. Power-on and brown-out thresholds.) '
() '
(  Parameter                      Symbol   Min    Typ    Max    Unit) '
(  Power-on reset threshold       VPOR     2.70   2.80   2.90   V) '
(  Brown-out reset threshold      VBOR     2.50   2.60   2.70   V) '
(  Brown-out filter time          tBOR     1      2      4      \265s) '
(  Reset to I\262C ready             tRDY            1.5    2.0    ms) '
(  Reset to first valid angle     tVAL            2.0    2.5    ms) '
() '
(## 11.4 Important: brown-out resets are silent) '
() '
(A brown-out reset can be shorter than one host control period. The host) '
(does not see a bus error and the angle keeps arriving after 2 ms. The only) '
(evidence is STATUS.RST, which stays set until the host reads STATUS \(page) '
(33\). The configuration written by the host at start-up is lost: CONF2 is) '
(back to 0x00 and the noise filter is off. Errata E-3 on page 58 describes) '
(the consequences and the required workaround.) '
ET
BT /F1 7.5 Tf 200.25 36 Td (ENCX14-RM rev 1.2    CC-BY-4.0    page 11 of 60) Tj ET
endstream
endobj
27 0 obj
<< /Type /Page /Parent 2 0 R /MediaBox [0 0 612 792] /Resources << /Font << /F1 3 0 R >> >> /Contents 28 0 R >>
endobj
28 0 obj
<< /Length 2269 >>
stream
BT /F1 9 Tf 11 TL 54 740 Td
(# 12 Timing, read latency and phase lag) '
() '
(## 12.1 Internal timing) '
() '
(  Parameter                                 Typ     Max     Unit) '
(  Internal sampling period \(8 kHz\)          125     125     \265s) '
(  CORDIC processing delay                   60      80      \265s) '
(  Register update period \(ANGLE, RAW\)       125     125     \265s) '
() '
(## 12.2 Read latency seen by a host) '
() '
(The angle that a host receives is older than the moment the read) '
(completes. The age is the sum of the internal processing delay, the time) '
(since the last register update, the I\262C transfer time and the time the host) '
(firmware takes to service the transfer.) '
() '
(For the typical case of a host running a 1 kHz control loop that starts one) '
(3-byte burst read \(page 40\) per control period at 400 kHz, and that uses the) '
(value in the next control step, the effective latency is two control) '
(periods.) '
() '
(**The I\262C read latency is 2 ms for a host that polls once per 1 ms control) '
(period and uses the result in the following period.**) '
() '
(Model the encoder in simulation as a pure delay of 2 ms on the true angle,) '
(followed by the FILT filter and the output noise.) '
() '
(## 12.3 Phase lag) '
() '
(A pure delay T adds a phase lag of 360\260 x f x T at frequency f. The FILT) '
(filter adds atan\(2 x pi x f x tau\). Table 12-1 lists the total phase lag) '
(for the 2 ms latency case.) '
() '
(Table 12-1. Phase lag from a 2 ms delay plus the FILT time constant.) '
() '
(  Frequency   FILT=0     FILT=3 \(1 ms\)   FILT=5 \(4 ms\)   FILT=7 \(16 ms\)) '
(  1 Hz        0.7\260       1.1\260            2.2\260            6.5\260) '
(  4 Hz        2.9\260       4.3\260            8.6\260            24.8\260) '
(  10 Hz       7.2\260       10.8\260           21.3\260           52.4\260) '
(  30 Hz       21.6\260      32.3\260           58.6\260           93.2\260) '
() '
(A joint loop with a crossover between 3 and 10 Hz tolerates FILT = 3 with) '
(almost no extra phase lag. FILT = 7 costs about 25\260 at 4 Hz and can make a) '
(loop that was stable with FILT = 0 oscillate. See page 49.) '
ET
BT /F1 7.5 Tf 200.25 36 Td (ENCX14-RM rev 1.2    CC-BY-4.0    page 12 of 60) Tj ET
endstream
endobj
