38 0 obj
<< /Length 1722 >>
stream
BT /F1 9 Tf 11 TL 54 740 Td
(# 17 PCB layout guidance) '
() '
(## 17.1 Placement) '
() '
(  - Place the decoupling capacitors on the same layer as the encoder, as) '
(    close to pins 1 and 2 as possible.) '
(  - Route SDA and SCL as a pair, away from motor phase traces.) '
(  - Keep current-carrying traces at least 5 mm from the package. A trace) '
(    carrying 1 A at 5 mm produces about 40 \265T, which is small compared with) '
(    the 30 mT minimum signal, but motor phase currents of 10 A or more are) '
(    not.) '
() '
(## 17.2 Ground) '
() '
(Connect GND to a solid ground plane. Do not route the motor return current) '
(under the encoder.) '
() '
(## 17.3 Supply) '
() '
(If the board carries a motor driver, give the encoder its own supply branch) '
(from the regulator, with an RC filter \(10 Ohm, 10 \265F\). This reduces the) '
(probability of a brown-out reset when the motor starts \(page 53\). It does) '
(not remove that probability, so firmware must still handle STATUS.RST \(page) '
(45\).) '
() '
(## 17.4 Connectors and cables) '
() '
(When the encoder board is connected to the host by a cable longer than) '
(30 cm, use fast mode \(400 kHz\) rather than fast-mode plus, add 100 Ohm) '
(series resistors on SDA and SCL at the encoder side, and keep VDD and GND in) '
(the same cable as the bus. Long cables raise the chance of supply dips at) '
(the encoder.) '
() '
(## 17.5 Magnet keep-out) '
() '
(Keep ferromagnetic parts \(steel screws, shields\) at least 5 mm away from the) '
(magnet. Ferromagnetic parts distort the field and add an angle error that) '
(repeats once per revolution \(page 27\).) '
ET
BT /F1 7.5 Tf 200.25 36 Td (ENCX14-RM rev 1.2    CC-BY-4.0    page 17 of 60) Tj ET
endstream
endobj
39 0 obj
<< /Type /Page /Parent 2 0 R /MediaBox [0 0 612 792] /Resources << /Font << /F1 3 0 R >> >> /Contents 40 0 R >>
endobj
40 0 obj
<< /Length 1669 >>
stream
BT /F1 9 Tf 11 TL 54 740 Td
(# 18 Soldering and handling) '
() '
(## 18.1 Reflow profile) '
() '
(The device is qualified for lead-free reflow according to the following) '
(profile:) '
() '
(  Stage                              Value) '
(  Preheat, 150 to 200 \260C             60 to 120 s) '
(  Time above 217 \260C                  60 to 150 s) '
(  Peak temperature                   260 \260C, max 10 s within 5 \260C of peak) '
(  Ramp-up rate                       3 \260C/s max) '
(  Ramp-down rate                     6 \260C/s max) '
(  Maximum number of reflows          3) '
() '
(## 18.2 Moisture sensitivity) '
() '
(The device is MSL 3. After opening the dry pack, mount within 168 hours at) '
(30 \260C and 60 % RH or less, or bake at 125 \260C for 24 hours.) '
() '
(## 18.3 Hand soldering) '
() '
(Hand soldering is allowed with a soldering iron at up to 350 \260C for 3 s per) '
(lead. Do not touch the top of the package with the iron; the mould compound) '
(above the Hall array is thin.) '
() '
(## 18.4 Cleaning) '
() '
(Use no-clean flux where possible. If cleaning is needed, use isopropyl) '
(alcohol. Do not use ultrasonic cleaning; it can crack the die attach.) '
() '
(## 18.5 Handling the magnet) '
() '
(NdFeB magnets are brittle and attract each other strongly. Store magnets) '
(apart from encoders and from each other. Do not machine NdFeB magnets.) '
() '
(## 18.6 OTP and soldering) '
() '
(OTP contents are not affected by reflow. Program OTP after assembly \(page) '
(46\), because the zero position depends on the final mechanical mounting.) '
ET
BT /F1 7.5 Tf 200.25 36 Td (ENCX14-RM rev 1.2    CC-BY-4.0    page 18 of 60) Tj ET
endstream
endobj
41 0 obj
<< /Type /Page /Parent 2 0 R /MediaBox [0 0 612 792] /Resources << /Font << /F1 3 0 R >> >> /Contents 42 0 R >>
endobj
42 0 obj
<< /Length 1606 >>
stream
BT /F1 9 Tf 11 TL 54 740 Td
(# 19 Magnet selection) '
() '
(## 19.1 Magnet type) '
() '
(Use a diametrically magnetised cylindrical magnet. The north and south) '
(poles are on opposite halves of the cylinder, not on its end faces. An) '
(axially magnetised magnet does not work with this device.) '
() '
(## 19.2 Recommended magnets) '
() '
(Table 19-1. Recommended magnets.) '
() '
(  Material      Diameter   Height   Air gap range   Notes) '
(  NdFeB N35     6 mm       2.5 mm   0.5 to 3.0 mm   Reference magnet) '
(  NdFeB N35     8 mm       3.0 mm   1.0 to 4.0 mm   Larger air gap) '
(  NdFeB N35     4 mm       2.0 mm   0.5 to 1.8 mm   Small joints) '
(  SmCo 2:17     6 mm       2.5 mm   0.5 to 2.4 mm   High temperature) '
() '
(## 19.3 Temperature) '
() '
(The remanence of NdFeB falls by about 0.12 % per \260C. Between 25 \260C and) '
(125 \260C the field falls by about 12 %. The AGC \(page 35\) compensates for the) '
(loss automatically as long as the field stays above 30 mT. Check the field) '
(at the highest operating temperature and largest air gap.) '
() '
(## 19.4 Magnet holder) '
() '
(Fix the magnet in a non-ferromagnetic holder \(brass, aluminium, plastic\).) '
(Use an adhesive that keeps the magnet centred to within 0.1 mm. A magnet) '
(that shifts in its holder causes a slowly growing angle error.) '
() '
(## 19.5 Magnet and noise) '
() '
(Output noise depends on the field. At 30 mT the raw noise is about 20 %) '
(higher than at 60 mT. Table 31-2 is measured at 60 mT. See page 26.) '
ET
BT /F1 7.5 Tf 200.25 36 Td (ENCX14-RM rev 1.2    CC-BY-4.0    page 19 of 60) Tj ET
endstream
endobj
43 0 obj
<< /Type /Page /Parent 2 0 R /MediaBox [0 0 612 792] /Resources << /Font << /F1 3 0 R >> >> /Contents 44 0 R >>
endobj
44 0 obj
<< /Length 1810 >>
stream
BT /F1 9 Tf 11 TL 54 740 Td
(# 20 Air gap and field strength) '
() '
(## 20.1 Field versus air gap) '
() '
(Table 20-1 gives the field at the Hall array for the reference magnet) '
(\(NdFeB N35, 6 mm x 2.5 mm\) as a function of the mechanical air gap tAIR,) '
(measured from the top of the package to the face of the magnet.) '
() '
(Table 20-1. Field versus air gap, reference magnet, 25 \260C.) '
() '
(  tAIR \(mm\)    Field \(mT\)   AGC \(typ\)   Status flags) '
(  0.25         121          0           MAGH) '
(  0.50         98           12          MAGH \(marginal\)) '
(  0.75         88           24          -) '
(  1.00         79           41          -) '
(  1.50         62           78          -) '
(  2.00         48           112         -) '
(  2.50         37           151         -) '
(  3.00         30           186         MAGL \(marginal\)) '
(  3.50         24           220         MAGL) '
(  4.00         19           255         MAGL) '
() '
(Rev 1.1 corrected the tAIR maximum from 3.5 mm to 3.0 mm.) '
() '
(## 20.2 Choosing the air gap) '
() '
(Choose a nominal air gap in the middle of the valid field range so that) '
(tolerances and temperature drift keep the field within 30 to 90 mT. For the) '
(reference magnet, 1.5 mm is the recommended nominal value.) '
() '
(## 20.3 Field and noise) '
() '
(The output noise in table 31-2 is specified at 60 mT. At 30 mT add 20 %.) '
(At 90 mT subtract 5 %. These factors apply to all FILT settings.) '
() '
(## 20.4 Axial play) '
() '
(Axial play of the shaft changes the air gap. A play of \2610.2 mm around a) '
(1.5 mm nominal gap changes the field by about \2617 mT, which is well within) '
(the AGC range and has no effect on the angle.) '
ET
BT /F1 7.5 Tf 200.25 36 Td (ENCX14-RM rev 1.2    CC-BY-4.0    page 20 of 60) Tj ET
endstream
endobj
45 0 obj
<< /Type /Page /Parent 2 0 R /MediaBox [0 0 612 792] /Resources << /Font << /F1 3 0 R >> >> /Contents 46 0 R >>
endobj
46 0 obj
<< /Length 1690 >>
stream
BT /F1 9 Tf 11 TL 54 740 Td
(# 21 Mechanical alignment) '
() '
(## 21.1 Lateral displacement) '
() '
(The magnet axis should coincide with the centre of the Hall array \(page) '
(16\). Lateral displacement causes an angle error that repeats once per) '
(revolution.) '
() '
(  Lateral displacement    Peak angle error \(reference magnet, 1.5 mm gap\)) '
(  0.10 mm                 0.10\260) '
(  0.25 mm                 0.28\260) '
(  0.50 mm                 0.62\260) '
(  1.00 mm                 1.50\260) '
() '
(## 21.2 Tilt) '
() '
(Tilt of the magnet relative to the package surface causes an angle error) '
(that repeats twice per revolution.) '
() '
(  Tilt      Peak angle error) '
(  1\260        0.05\260) '
(  2\260        0.12\260) '
(  5\260        0.35\260) '
() '
(## 21.3 Run-out) '
() '
(Shaft run-out moves the magnet in a circle. The error is the same as a) '
(lateral displacement equal to the run-out radius, but its phase rotates with) '
(the shaft.) '
() '
(## 21.4 Alignment procedure) '
() '
(1. Mount the encoder board and the magnet holder.) '
(2. Rotate the shaft slowly through one revolution while reading ANGLE.) '
(3. Compare ANGLE with a reference \(a second encoder or an optical index\).) '
(4. Fit a sine at once and twice the rotation frequency to the difference.) '
(5. If the once-per-revolution amplitude exceeds the budget, re-centre the) '
(   board.) '
() '
(## 21.5 Effect on servo loops) '
() '
(Alignment errors are smooth and repeat with the shaft angle. They appear as) '
(a slow position error, not as noise. They are not reduced by FILT \(page 31\).) '
ET
BT /F1 7.5 Tf 200.25 36 Td (ENCX14-RM rev 1.2    CC-BY-4.0    page 21 of 60) Tj ET
endstream
endobj
47 0 obj
<< /Type /Page /Parent 2 0 R /MediaBox [0 0 612 792] /Resources << /Font << /F1 3 0 R >> >> /Contents 48 0 R >>
endobj
48 0 obj
<< /Length 1794 >>
stream
BT /F1 9 Tf 11 TL 54 740 Td
(# 22 Alignment error and its effect on accuracy) '
() '
(## 22.1 Error budget example) '
() '
(For a robot elbow joint with the reference magnet at a 1.5 mm gap:) '
() '
(  Source                               Peak error) '
(  Lateral displacement 0.25 mm         0.28\260) '
(  Tilt 1\260                              0.05\260) '
(  Integral non-linearity \(page 27\)     0.10\260) '
(  Stray field from motor \(5 mT\)        0.15\260) '
(  Total \(root sum of squares\)          0.35\260) '
() '
(At the end of a 300 mm forearm, 0.35\260 corresponds to 1.8 mm. This is a slow) '
(error that depends on the joint angle. It does not cause oscillation.) '
() '
(## 22.2 Noise is a separate budget) '
() '
(Output noise is random and broadband \(page 26\). With FILT = 0 the noise is) '
(12 LSB RMS = 0.26\260 RMS. At the end of a 300 mm forearm this is 1.4 mm RMS of) '
(apparent position noise. A position loop with a high proportional gain and a) '
(derivative term turns this noise into motor torque, which can be seen as) '
(vibration of the arm. The noise budget is set by FILT, not by alignment.) '
() '
(## 22.3 Calibration) '
() '
(Alignment errors can be calibrated out with a look-up table in the host) '
(firmware. Noise cannot be calibrated out; it can only be filtered \(page 31,) '
(page 49\).) '
() '
(## 22.4 Summary) '
() '
(  Error type          Varies with           Reduced by) '
(  Alignment           shaft angle           mechanical fix, calibration) '
(  Stray field         motor current         shielding, distance) '
(  Non-linearity       shaft angle           calibration) '
(  Output noise        nothing \(random\)      FILT[2:0], host filtering) '
ET
BT /F1 7.5 Tf 200.25 36 Td (ENCX14-RM rev 1.2    CC-BY-4.0    page 22 of 60) Tj ET
endstream
endobj
49 0 obj
<< /Type /Page /Parent 2 0 R /MediaBox [0 0 612 792] /Resources << /Font << /F1 3 0 R >> >> /Contents 50 0 R >>
endobj
50 0 obj
<< /Length 1791 >>
stream
BT /F1 9 Tf 11 TL 54 740 Td
(# 23 Signal chain overview) '
() '
(The signal chain converts the magnetic field into a 14-bit angle in five) '
(stages:) '
() '
(  1. Hall array. Four Hall plates measure the two field components Bx, By.) '
(  2. Front end. Differential amplifiers with automatic gain control.) '
(  3. ADC. Two 12-bit sigma-delta converters sampled at 8 kHz.) '
(  4. CORDIC. Computes atan2\(By, Bx\) to 14 bits once per sample.) '
(  5. FILT. A first-order IIR low-pass filter on the angle \(page 31\).) '
() '
(After the filter, the output stage applies ZPOS, MPOS, DIR and HYST and) '
(updates ANGLE and RAW once every 125 \265s.) '
() '
(## 23.1 Angle wrap handling in the filter) '
() '
(The filter works on the unwrapped angle, so a transition from code 16383 to) '
(code 0 does not produce a filtered spike. The output is wrapped again to 14) '
(bits after the filter.) '
() '
(## 23.2 Where the noise comes from) '
() '
(Most of the output noise comes from the Hall plates and the front end) '
(\(thermal and 1/f noise\) and from ADC quantisation. CORDIC rounding adds less) '
(than 0.3 LSB RMS. Page 26 gives the breakdown.) '
() '
(## 23.3 Where the delay comes from) '
() '
(  Stage                      Delay) '
(  ADC conversion             40 \265s) '
(  CORDIC                     20 \265s) '
(  FILT \(when enabled\)        tau = 2^n / 8000 s, see table 31-2) '
(  Register update            0 to 125 \265s \(sampling phase\)) '
(  I\262C transfer               see page 14) '
(  Host scheduling            depends on the host \(page 12\)) '
() '
(The total delay seen by a typical 1 kHz host is 2 ms \(page 12\), plus the) '
(FILT time constant if the filter is enabled.) '
ET
BT /F1 7.5 Tf 200.25 36 Td (ENCX14-RM rev 1.2    CC-BY-4.0    page 23 of 60) Tj ET
endstream
endobj
51 0 obj
<< /Type /Page /Parent 2 0 R /MediaBox [0 0 612 792] /Resources << /Font << /F1 3 0 R >> >> /Contents 52 0 R >>
endobj
52 0 obj
<< /Length 1714 >>
stream
BT /F1 9 Tf 11 TL 54 740 Td
(# 24 Hall array and front end) '
() '
(## 24.1 Hall array) '
() '
(Four Hall plates are arranged on a circle of 1.0 mm radius at 0\260, 90\260,) '
(180\260 and 270\260. Opposite plates are subtracted to cancel any uniform) '
(external field along the package normal. The difference signals are) '
(proportional to Bx and By of the magnet field.) '
() '
(## 24.2 Common-mode field rejection) '
() '
(A uniform external field \(for example the field of a nearby motor\) is) '
(rejected by the differential arrangement with a ratio of about 40 dB. A) '
(non-uniform stray field is not rejected and appears as an angle error \(page) '
(22\).) '
() '
(## 24.3 Automatic gain control) '
() '
(The AGC adjusts the front-end gain so that the amplitude of the Bx/By) '
(signals at the ADC input is constant. The AGC value \(0 to 255\) is readable) '
(in register AGC \(0x1A, page 35\). A low AGC value means a strong field; a) '
(high value means a weak field.) '
() '
(The AGC loop time constant is 10 ms. The AGC does not affect the angle) '
(directly, but a fast change of air gap \(axial shock\) can cause a short) '
(angle disturbance while the AGC settles.) '
() '
(## 24.4 Front-end noise) '
() '
(The input-referred noise of the front end is 2.1 \265T RMS in the 4 kHz) '
(bandwidth of the ADC. Together with the Hall plate noise and quantisation,) '
(this gives the 12 LSB RMS total output noise at FILT = 0 \(page 26\).) '
() '
(## 24.5 Front-end saturation) '
() '
(Above 120 mT the front end saturates. MAGH is set. The angle is invalid) '
(while MAGH is set and must not be used for control.) '
ET
BT /F1 7.5 Tf 200.25 36 Td (ENCX14-RM rev 1.2    CC-BY-4.0    page 24 of 60) Tj ET
endstream
endobj
53 0 obj
<< /Type /Page /Parent 2 0 R /MediaBox [0 0 612 792] /Resources << /Font << /F1 3 0 R >> >> /Contents 54 0 R >>
endobj
54 0 obj
<< /Length 1579 >>
stream
BT /F1 9 Tf 11 TL 54 740 Td
(# 25 ADC and CORDIC angle computation) '
() '
(## 25.1 ADC) '
() '
(Two sigma-delta ADCs convert Bx and By simultaneously at 8 kHz. Each) '
(conversion has 12 effective bits. Simultaneous sampling avoids an angle) '
(error that would otherwise grow with rotational speed.) '
() '
(## 25.2 CORDIC) '
() '
(The CORDIC engine computes the angle atan2\(By, Bx\) in 16 iterations and) '
(rounds the result to 14 bits. The CORDIC result is available 60 \265s after) '
(the start of the ADC sample \(page 23\).) '
() '
(## 25.3 Output resolution versus noise) '
() '
(The output has 14 bits \(0.022\260 per LSB\). With FILT = 0 the output noise is) '
(12 LSB RMS, so only about 7.7 bits are noise-free in a single read. The) '
(FILT filter trades bandwidth for noise \(page 31\):) '
() '
(  FILT     Noise \(LSB RMS\)   Noise-free bits \(approx.\)) '
(  0        12.0              7.7) '
(  3        3.10              9.6) '
(  5        1.51              10.7) '
(  7        0.75              11.7) '
() '
(Noise-free bits are computed as 14 - log2\(6.6 x noise\), where 6.6 x RMS is) '
(the peak-to-peak noise for a Gaussian distribution at 99.9 % coverage.) '
() '
(## 25.4 Linearity) '
() '
(The CORDIC engine is exact to within 0.25 LSB. Non-linearity is dominated) '
(by magnetic effects \(page 27\).) '
() '
(## 25.5 Output data rate) '
() '
(ANGLE and RAW are updated every 125 \265s \(8 kHz\). A host that reads faster) '
(than 8 kHz receives repeated values.) '
ET
BT /F1 7.5 Tf 200.25 36 Td (ENCX14-RM rev 1.2    CC-BY-4.0    page 25 of 60) Tj ET
endstream
endobj
55 0 obj
<< /Type /Page /Parent 2 0 R /MediaBox [0 0 612 792] /Resources << /Font << /F1 3 0 R >> >> /Contents 56 0 R >>
endobj
56 0 obj
<< /Length 1612 >>
stream
BT /F1 9 Tf 11 TL 54 740 Td
(# 26 Noise sources) '
() '
(## 26.1 Noise breakdown at FILT = 0, 60 mT, 25 \260C) '
() '
(  Source                              Contribution \(LSB RMS\)) '
(  Hall plate thermal noise            9.8) '
(  Front-end amplifier noise           6.2) '
(  ADC quantisation                    2.4) '
(  Supply-coupled noise \(with 4.7 \265F\)  1.1) '
(  CORDIC rounding                     0.3) '
(  Total \(root sum of squares\)         12.0) '
() '
(## 26.2 Spectrum) '
() '
(With FILT = 0 the noise is white from DC to 4 kHz \(half the 8 kHz sampling) '
(rate\). A host that samples the angle at 1 kHz sees the whole 0 to 4 kHz) '
(noise power aliased into its 0 to 500 Hz band. The RMS value per read is) '
(therefore still 12 LSB.) '
() '
(## 26.3 Effect of FILT) '
() '
(FILT is a first-order low-pass filter running at 8 kHz. It reduces the) '
(bandwidth of the white noise and therefore its RMS value. The noise gain of) '
(the filter is sqrt\(alpha / \(2 - alpha\)\), where alpha = 2^-n \(page 31\).) '
() '
(## 26.4 Dependence on field) '
() '
(  Field     Noise multiplier) '
(  30 mT     1.20) '
(  45 mT     1.08) '
(  60 mT     1.00) '
(  90 mT     0.95) '
() '
(## 26.5 Dependence on temperature) '
() '
(The noise increases by about 0.1 % per \260C above 25 \260C, so by about 10 % at) '
(125 \260C.) '
() '
(## 26.6 Noise is not drift) '
() '
(Noise as specified here is the fast, random part of the error. Slow) '
(variations with temperature or shaft angle are covered on page 27.) '
ET
BT /F1 7.5 Tf 200.25 36 Td (ENCX14-RM rev 1.2    CC-BY-4.0    page 26 of 60) Tj ET
endstream
endobj
57 0 obj
<< /Type /Page /Parent 2 0 R /MediaBox [0 0 612 792] /Resources << /Font << /F1 3 0 R >> >> /Contents 58 0 R >>
endobj
58 0 obj
<< /Length 1790 >>
stream
BT /F1 9 Tf 11 TL 54 740 Td
(# 27 Accuracy and linearity) '
() '
(## 27.1 Definitions) '
() '
(  INL     Integral non-linearity: the largest deviation of the output from a) '
(          straight line through the full revolution, after removing offset.) '
(  Drift   Change of the output at a fixed angle over temperature.) '
(  Noise   Random variation of consecutive reads at a fixed angle \(page 26\).) '
() '
(## 27.2 Specifications) '
() '
(Table 27-1. Accuracy \(reference magnet, 1.5 mm air gap, perfect alignment\).) '
() '
(  Parameter                         Typ       Max       Unit) '
(  INL, 25 \260C                        0.10      0.25      \260) '
(  INL, -40 to 125 \260C                0.15      0.40      \260) '
(  Zero drift, -40 to 125 \260C         0.05      0.12      \260) '
(  Hysteresis \(HYST = 0\)             0         0         LSB) '
(  Output noise, FILT = 0            12.0      14.0      LSB RMS) '
(  Output noise, FILT = 3            3.10      3.6       LSB RMS) '
(  Output noise, FILT = 7            0.75      0.9       LSB RMS) '
() '
(## 27.3 Notes) '
() '
(1. INL includes the CORDIC error and the effect of magnet inhomogeneity. It) '
(   excludes alignment errors \(page 21\).) '
(2. Noise values are at 60 mT. See page 26 for other field values.) '
(3. Max noise values are the 3-sigma production limits.) '
() '
(## 27.4 Improving accuracy) '
() '
(  - Calibrate the INL with a look-up table in the host.) '
(  - Use FILT to reduce noise, at the cost of phase lag \(page 12\).) '
(  - Average several reads in the host only if the host reads faster than) '
(    the ANGLE update rate; otherwise averaging repeated values does nothing) '
(    \(page 25\).) '
ET
BT /F1 7.5 Tf 200.25 36 Td (ENCX14-RM rev 1.2    CC-BY-4.0    page 27 of 60) Tj ET
endstream
endobj
