64 0 obj
<< /Length 1749 >>
stream
BT /F1 9 Tf 11 TL 54 740 Td
(# 30 ZPOS and MPOS \(zero and maximum position\)) '
() '
(## 30.1 ZPOS \(0x03-0x04, RW, OTP shadow\)) '
() '
(  Register   Bits   Field         Description) '
(  ZPOS_H     5:0    ZPOS[13:8]    Zero position, high bits) '
(  ZPOS_L     7:0    ZPOS[7:0]     Zero position, low bits) '
() '
(ZPOS is subtracted from the filtered angle before it is written to ANGLE.) '
(Write ZPOS_H first, then ZPOS_L. The new zero takes effect after the write) '
(to ZPOS_L.) '
() '
(## 30.2 MPOS \(0x05-0x06, RW, OTP shadow\)) '
() '
(  Register   Bits   Field         Description) '
(  MPOS_H     5:0    MPOS[13:8]    Maximum position, high bits) '
(  MPOS_L     7:0    MPOS[7:0]     Maximum position, low bits) '
() '
(When MPOS is less than 0x3FFF, ANGLE is scaled so that the range from ZPOS) '
(to MPOS spans the full 14-bit output. The default 0x3FFF gives a full) '
(360\260 range with no scaling.) '
() '
(## 30.3 Scaling and noise) '
() '
(Scaling a small range to the full output also scales the noise. For a) '
(90\260 range \(MPOS = 4096\) the noise in ANGLE is four times the noise in RAW,) '
(expressed in output codes. Noise expressed in degrees is unchanged.) '
() '
(## 30.4 Programming ZPOS and MPOS permanently) '
() '
(ZPOS and MPOS written over I\262C are volatile until burnt into OTP. A reset) '
(reloads them from OTP. To make them permanent, use the OTP burn command on) '
(page 36. OTP can be burnt only once.) '
() '
(## 30.5 Host-side alternative) '
() '
(Many hosts keep ZPOS = 0 and MPOS = 0x3FFF and apply the offset in) '
(firmware. This avoids OTP programming in production and avoids surprises) '
(after a reset.) '
ET
BT /F1 7.5 Tf 200.25 36 Td (ENCX14-RM rev 1.2    CC-BY-4.0    page 30 of 60) Tj ET
endstream
endobj
65 0 obj
<< /Type /Page /Parent 2 0 R /MediaBox [0 0 612 792] /Resources << /Font << /F1 3 0 R >> >> /Contents 66 0 R >>
endobj
66 0 obj
<< /Length 2806 >>
stream
BT /F1 9 Tf 11 TL 54 740 Td
(# 31 CONF2 and the FILT[2:0] filter) '
() '
(## 31.1 CONF2 \(0x08, RW, volatile\)) '
() '
(Table 31-1. CONF2 bit fields.) '
() '
(  Bits   Field       Default   Description) '
(  7      -           0         Reserved, write 0) '
(  6      WD          0         Watchdog enable \(page 32, page 54\)) '
(  5:4    HYST[1:0]   0         Output hysteresis, 0 to 3 LSB \(page 32\)) '
(  3      DIR         0         Direction: 0 = clockwise increases \(page 32\)) '
(  2:0    FILT[2:0]   0         Output noise filter setting n, 0 = off) '
() '
(CONF2 has no OTP shadow. Its value after any reset is 0x00, so FILT = 0 and) '
(the filter is off. This includes brown-out resets \(errata E-3, page 58\).) '
() '
(## 31.2 The FILT filter) '
() '
(FILT selects a first-order IIR low-pass filter that runs on the angle at the) '
(8 kHz internal sample rate, before the RAW and ANGLE registers:) '
() '
(    y[k] = y[k-1] + alpha x \(x[k] - y[k-1]\),   alpha = 2^-n,  n = FILT[2:0]) '
() '
(With n = 0 the filter is bypassed. The time constant is tau = 2^n / 8000 s) '
(and the -3 dB bandwidth is approximately 1 / \(2 x pi x tau\). For white input) '
(noise the output noise is multiplied by sqrt\(alpha / \(2 - alpha\)\).) '
() '
(Table 31-2. Output noise and bandwidth versus FILT \(60 mT, 25 \260C\).) '
() '
(  FILT n   alpha    tau        Noise \(LSB RMS\)   Noise \(\260 RMS\)   Approx. BW) '
(  0        1        off        12.0              0.264           -) '
(  1        1/2      0.25 ms    6.93              0.152           637 Hz) '
(  2        1/4      0.5 ms     4.54              0.100           318 Hz) '
(  3        1/8      1 ms       3.10              0.068           159 Hz) '
(  4        1/16     2 ms       2.155             0.047           79.6 Hz) '
(  5        1/32     4 ms       1.51              0.033           39.8 Hz) '
(  6        1/64     8 ms       1.06              0.023           19.9 Hz) '
(  7        1/128    16 ms      0.75              0.016           9.9 Hz) '
() '
(## 31.3 Writing FILT) '
() '
(To set FILT = 3 with the other CONF2 fields at their defaults, write 0x03 to) '
(address 0x08. To change FILT without changing other fields, read CONF2,) '
(clear bits 2..0, OR in the new value and write it back.) '
() '
(## 31.4 Important) '
() '
(  - FILT = 0 after every reset. A host that needs the filter must write) '
(    CONF2 at start-up and again after every reset \(page 45, page 58\).) '
(  - FILT adds phase lag \(table 12-1\). Do not choose a time constant that is) '
(    large compared with the period of the fastest motion you control.) '
(  - If ADCSLOW = 1 \(page 29\), the filter runs at 4 kHz and every tau in) '
(    table 31-2 doubles.) '
ET
BT /F1 7.5 Tf 200.25 36 Td (ENCX14-RM rev 1.2    CC-BY-4.0    page 31 of 60) Tj ET
endstream
endobj
67 0 obj
<< /Type /Page /Parent 2 0 R /MediaBox [0 0 612 792] /Resources << /Font << /F1 3 0 R >> >> /Contents 68 0 R >>
endobj
68 0 obj
<< /Length 1725 >>
stream
BT /F1 9 Tf 11 TL 54 740 Td
(# 32 CONF2 other fields \(DIR, HYST, WD\)) '
() '
(## 32.1 DIR \(CONF2 bit 3\)) '
() '
(  DIR = 0   angle increases for clockwise rotation, seen from the top) '
(  DIR = 1   angle increases for counter-clockwise rotation) '
() '
(DIR is volatile. After a reset it returns to 0. A host that sets DIR = 1) '
(must set it again after every reset \(page 15, page 58\).) '
() '
(## 32.2 HYST[1:0] \(CONF2 bits 5..4\)) '
() '
(  HYST    Hysteresis on ANGLE) '
(  0       none) '
(  1       1 LSB) '
(  2       2 LSB) '
(  3       3 LSB) '
() '
(Hysteresis suppresses toggling of the last bits when the shaft is at rest.) '
(It is applied after FILT and does not reduce noise in a moving shaft.) '
() '
(Important: hysteresis is not a noise filter for servo loops. With HYST > 0) '
(the output moves in steps, which a derivative term turns into spikes. Use) '
(FILT instead \(page 49\). Hysteresis does not affect RAW.) '
() '
(## 32.3 WD \(CONF2 bit 6\)) '
() '
(  WD = 0   watchdog disabled \(default\)) '
(  WD = 1   watchdog enabled; the device resets itself if the internal) '
(           oscillator stalls for more than 1 ms \(page 54\)) '
() '
(Because the watchdog reset is also a reset, it clears CONF2, including WD) '
(itself. The host sees STATUS.RST = 1 after a watchdog reset \(page 33\).) '
() '
(## 32.4 Example values) '
() '
(  Purpose                                   CONF2 value) '
(  Defaults \(after any reset\)                0x00) '
(  FILT = 3, everything else default         0x03) '
(  FILT = 3, DIR = 1                         0x0B) '
(  FILT = 5, HYST = 1, WD = 1                0x55) '
ET
BT /F1 7.5 Tf 200.25 36 Td (ENCX14-RM rev 1.2    CC-BY-4.0    page 32 of 60) Tj ET
endstream
endobj
