70 0 obj
<< /Length 1886 >>
stream
BT /F1 9 Tf 11 TL 54 740 Td
(# 33 STATUS register) '
() '
(## 33.1 STATUS \(0x0B, RC\)) '
() '
(Table 33-1. STATUS bit fields.) '
() '
(  Bits   Field   Type   Description) '
(  7:5    -       R      Reserved, read as 0) '
(  4      OVF     RC     CORDIC overflow \(field too weak to compute an angle\)) '
(  3      MD      R      Magnet detected \(field between 20 mT and 120 mT\)) '
(  2      MAGH    R      Field too strong \(> 90 mT\)) '
(  1      MAGL    R      Field too weak \(< 30 mT\)) '
(  0      RST     RC     A reset has occurred since STATUS was last read) '
() '
(## 33.2 The RST flag is sticky) '
() '
(RST is set by every reset: power-on, brown-out, watchdog and soft reset) '
(\(page 11\). It stays set until the host reads STATUS. Reading STATUS clears) '
(RST and OVF. The value returned by that read still shows RST = 1; the flag) '
(is cleared after the read completes.) '
() '
(This means:) '
() '
(  - The host sees RST = 1 exactly once per reset \(or once for several resets) '
(    that happen between two reads\).) '
(  - The host must act on RST in the same control step in which it reads it.) '
(    The next read returns RST = 0 even though the configuration is still) '
(    lost.) '
(  - RST = 1 means CONF2 is 0x00 again: FILT is off, DIR = 0, HYST = 0,) '
(    WD = 0 \(errata E-3, page 58\).) '
() '
(## 33.3 Reading STATUS together with the angle) '
() '
(The recommended way to read STATUS is the 3-byte burst read starting at) '
(0x0B \(page 40\). It returns STATUS, ANGLE_H and ANGLE_L in one transaction,) '
(so the host checks RST on every control step at no extra cost.) '
() '
(## 33.4 MAGL, MAGH and MD) '
() '
(These flags reflect the current field. They are not sticky. The angle is) '
(not valid when MAGL or MAGH is set or MD is clear. See page 52.) '
ET
BT /F1 7.5 Tf 200.25 36 Td (ENCX14-RM rev 1.2    CC-BY-4.0    page 33 of 60) Tj ET
endstream
endobj
71 0 obj
<< /Type /Page /Parent 2 0 R /MediaBox [0 0 612 792] /Resources << /Font << /F1 3 0 R >> >> /Contents 72 0 R >>
endobj
72 0 obj
<< /Length 1656 >>
stream
BT /F1 9 Tf 11 TL 54 740 Td
(# 34 ANGLE and RAW output registers) '
() '
(## 34.1 ANGLE \(0x0C-0x0D, R\)) '
() '
(  Register   Bits   Field          Description) '
(  ANGLE_H    7:6    -              Read as 0) '
(  ANGLE_H    5:0    ANGLE[13:8]    Scaled angle, high bits) '
(  ANGLE_L    7:0    ANGLE[7:0]     Scaled angle, low bits) '
() '
(ANGLE is the filtered angle after ZPOS, MPOS, DIR and HYST.) '
() '
(## 34.2 RAW \(0x0E-0x0F, R\)) '
() '
(  Register   Bits   Field          Description) '
(  RAW_H      7:6    -              Read as 0) '
(  RAW_H      5:0    RAW[13:8]      Raw angle, high bits) '
(  RAW_L      7:0    RAW[7:0]       Raw angle, low bits) '
() '
(RAW is the filtered angle before ZPOS, MPOS, DIR and HYST. FILT does act on) '
(RAW: RAW is taken after the filter \(page 6\).) '
() '
(## 34.3 Data consistency) '
() '
(A multi-byte read latches the high and low bytes together at the start of) '
(the read, so ANGLE_H and ANGLE_L \(or RAW_H and RAW_L\) always belong to the) '
(same sample when they are read in one transaction. Two separate single-byte) '
(reads can combine bytes from different samples.) '
() '
(## 34.4 Converting to radians) '
() '
(    angle_rad = code x 2 x pi / 16384) '
(    angle_deg = code x 360 / 16384) '
() '
(One code is 383.5 \265rad = 0.02197\260.) '
() '
(## 34.5 Unwrapping) '
() '
(The output wraps from 16383 to 0. For a joint that moves less than half a) '
(revolution between two reads, unwrap by adding or subtracting 16384 when) '
(the difference between consecutive reads exceeds 8192 in magnitude.) '
ET
BT /F1 7.5 Tf 200.25 36 Td (ENCX14-RM rev 1.2    CC-BY-4.0    page 34 of 60) Tj ET
endstream
endobj
