stream
BT /F1 9 Tf 11 TL 54 740 Td
(# 37 I\262C protocol overview) '
() '
(## 37.1 Bus parameters) '
() '
(  Address       0x4C \(ADDR = GND\) or 0x4D \(ADDR = VDD\), 7-bit) '
(  Speeds        100 kHz, 400 kHz, 1 MHz) '
(  Byte order    Most significant byte first) '
(  Pointer       8-bit register pointer, auto-increment on read and write) '
() '
(## 37.2 Transactions) '
() '
(  Transaction       Sequence) '
(  Write register    S, addr+W, ptr, data..., P) '
(  Read register     S, addr+W, ptr, Sr, addr+R, data..., P) '
(  Read current      S, addr+R, data..., P    \(uses the last pointer\)) '
() '
(  S = START, Sr = repeated START, P = STOP.) '
() '
(## 37.3 Pointer behaviour) '
() '
(The register pointer persists between transactions. After a read of N) '
(bytes starting at pointer p, the pointer is p + N \(with the wrap behaviour) '
(described on page 41\). A "read current" transaction therefore continues) '
(where the last read ended, which is rarely what a control loop wants. Always) '
(set the pointer explicitly.) '
() '
(## 37.4 Reset and the bus) '
() '
(During a reset the device releases SDA and does not respond to its address.) '
(A transaction that starts during the 1.5 ms after a reset is NACKed. After) '
(a brown-out the pointer is reset to 0x00.) '
() '
(## 37.5 Typical control-loop traffic) '
() '
(One 3-byte burst read from 0x0B per control period \(page 40\). Configuration) '
(writes only at start-up and after STATUS.RST \(page 45\).) '
ET
BT /F1 7.5 Tf 200.25 36 Td (ENCX14-RM rev 1.2    CC-BY-4.0    page 37 of 60) Tj ET
endstream
endobj
79 0 obj
<< /Type /Page /Parent 2 0 R /MediaBox [0 0 612 792] /Resources << /Font << /F1 3 0 R >> >> /Contents 80 0 R >>
endobj
80 0 obj
<< /Length 1308 >>
stream
BT /F1 9 Tf 11 TL 54 740 Td
(# 38 Write transaction) '
() '
(## 38.1 Sequence) '
() '
(  S | 0x98 \(0x4C + W\) | A | ptr | A | data0 | A | data1 | A | ... | P) '
() '
(Each data byte is written to the register at the pointer, and the pointer) '
(is incremented after every byte.) '
() '
(## 38.2 Example: enable the filter) '
() '
(Set FILT = 3 \(CONF2 = 0x03\):) '
() '
(  S | 0x98 | A | 0x08 | A | 0x03 | A | P) '
() '
(## 38.3 Example: set ZPOS to 0x1234) '
() '
(  S | 0x98 | A | 0x03 | A | 0x12 | A | 0x34 | A | P) '
() '
(## 38.4 Writes to read-only and reserved registers) '
() '
(Writes to read-only and reserved registers are acknowledged and ignored.) '
(The pointer is still incremented.) '
() '
(## 38.5 Write confirmation) '
() '
(The device does not report whether a write took effect. To confirm a) '
(configuration write, read the register back. A host that writes CONF2 and) '
(immediately reads it back can still lose the value later through a reset;) '
(read-back confirms the write, not that the value will stay \(page 58\).) '
() '
(## 38.6 Timing between writes) '
() '
(There is no minimum time between write transactions, except after a soft) '
(reset \(2 ms\) and during an OTP burn \(10 ms\).) '
ET
BT /F1 7.5 Tf 200.25 36 Td (ENCX14-RM rev 1.2    CC-BY-4.0    page 38 of 60) Tj ET
endstream
endobj
81 0 obj
<< /Type /Page /Parent 2 0 R /MediaBox [0 0 612 792] /Resources << /Font << /F1 3 0 R >> >> /Contents 82 0 R >>
endobj
82 0 obj
<< /Length 1060 >>
stream
BT /F1 9 Tf 11 TL 54 740 Td
(# 39 Read transaction) '
() '
(## 39.1 Sequence) '
() '
(  S | 0x98 | A | ptr | A | Sr | 0x99 | A | data0 | A | data1 | A | ... | NA | P) '
() '
(The host acknowledges every byte except the last, which it NACKs before the) '
(STOP.) '
() '
(## 39.2 Example: read CHIP_ID) '
() '
(  S | 0x98 | A | 0x00 | A | Sr | 0x99 | A | 0x14 | NA | P) '
() '
(## 39.3 Example: read ANGLE) '
() '
(  S | 0x98 | A | 0x0C | A | Sr | 0x99 | A | ANGLE_H | A | ANGLE_L | NA | P) '
() '
(The two bytes are latched together \(page 34\).) '
() '
(## 39.4 Reading STATUS clears flags) '
() '
(Any read transaction that includes address 0x0B clears STATUS.RST and) '
(STATUS.OVF after the byte has been transmitted \(page 33\). This includes) '
(burst reads that start below 0x0B and run through it.) '
() '
(## 39.5 Read latency) '
() '
(The data returned are the values latched at the start of the read. The) '
(latency seen by a typical host is given on page 12.) '
ET
BT /F1 7.5 Tf 200.25 36 Td (ENCX14-RM rev 1.2    CC-BY-4.0    page 39 of 60) Tj ET
endstream
endobj
83 0 obj
<< /Type /Page /Parent 2 0 R /MediaBox [0 0 612 792] /Resources << /Font << /F1 3 0 R >> >> /Contents 84 0 R >>
endobj
84 0 obj
<< /Length 1612 >>
stream
BT /F1 9 Tf 11 TL 54 740 Td
(# 40 Burst read of STATUS and ANGLE) '
() '
(## 40.1 The recommended per-cycle read) '
() '
(A 3-byte burst read starting at STATUS returns the status and the angle in) '
(one transaction:) '
() '
(  S | 0x98 | A | 0x0B | A | Sr | 0x99 | A | STATUS | A | ANGLE_H | A |) '
(  ANGLE_L | NA | P) '
() '
(  Byte 0   STATUS  \(RST, MAGL, MAGH, MD, OVF\)) '
(  Byte 1   ANGLE_H \(bits 13..8\)) '
(  Byte 2   ANGLE_L \(bits 7..0\)) '
() '
(STATUS and ANGLE are latched together. The read clears STATUS.RST \(page 33\).) '
() '
(## 40.2 Why read STATUS every cycle) '
() '
(  - RST tells the host that CONF2 has been cleared, so the filter is off and) '
(    DIR is 0 \(page 58\). The host must rewrite CONF2 in the same step.) '
(  - MAGL, MAGH and MD tell the host whether the angle is valid \(page 52\).) '
() '
(## 40.3 Host pseudocode) '
() '
(    every control period \(1 ms\):) '
(        status, angle = burst_read\(0x0B, 3\)) '
(        if status & 0x01:                 # STATUS.RST) '
(            write\(0x08, CONF2_VALUE\)      # restore FILT, DIR, HYST, WD) '
(        if \(status & 0x06\) or not \(status & 0x08\):) '
(            hold_last_command\(\)           # angle not valid \(page 52\)) '
(        else:) '
(            run_controller\(angle\)) '
() '
(## 40.4 Burst read of 4 bytes or more) '
() '
(A burst read that continues past ANGLE_L reads RAW_H and RAW_L \(0x0E-0x0F\).) '
(A burst read past 0x0F wraps incorrectly on all silicon revisions; see) '
(errata E-4 on page 59.) '
ET
BT /F1 7.5 Tf 200.25 36 Td (ENCX14-RM rev 1.2    CC-BY-4.0    page 40 of 60) Tj ET
endstream
endobj
85 0 obj
<< /Type /Page /Parent 2 0 R /MediaBox [0 0 612 792] /Resources << /Font << /F1 3 0 R >> >> /Contents 86 0 R >>
endobj
86 0 obj
<< /Length 1401 >>
stream
BT /F1 9 Tf 11 TL 54 740 Td
(# 41 Address auto-increment and wrap) '
() '
(## 41.1 Auto-increment) '
() '
(The register pointer increments after every byte in both read and write) '
(transactions.) '
() '
(## 41.2 Wrap rules) '
() '
(  Pointer after    Next pointer) '
(  0x0F             0x0B by design; 0x10 on all silicon \(errata E-4\)) '
(  0x1C             0x00) '
(  0x30             0x30 \(CMD does not auto-increment\)) '
(  reserved         next address) '
() '
(## 41.3 Intended wrap after 0x0F) '
() '
(The output block 0x0B-0x0F was designed to wrap back to STATUS \(0x0B\) so) '
(that a host could read STATUS, ANGLE and RAW continuously in one long) '
(burst. On all silicon the pointer moves to 0x10 instead \(errata E-4, page) '
(59\), and the byte read from 0x10 is undefined.) '
() '
(## 41.4 Recommended practice) '
() '
(  - Never rely on wrap. Start every transaction with an explicit pointer.) '
(  - Keep burst reads within 0x0B-0x0F.) '
(  - For long logging captures, issue one 5-byte burst read from 0x0B per) '
(    sample.) '
() '
(## 41.5 Write auto-increment across CONF2) '
() '
(A multi-byte write starting at 0x06 runs through the reserved address 0x07) '
(and into CONF2 at 0x08. Avoid writes that span 0x06-0x08; an unintended) '
(byte in CONF2 changes FILT, DIR, HYST or WD.) '
ET
BT /F1 7.5 Tf 200.25 36 Td (ENCX14-RM rev 1.2    CC-BY-4.0    page 41 of 60) Tj ET
endstream
endobj
87 0 obj
<< /Type /Page /Parent 2 0 R /MediaBox [0 0 612 792] /Resources << /Font << /F1 3 0 R >> >> /Contents 88 0 R >>
endobj
88 0 obj
<< /Length 1568 >>
stream
BT /F1 9 Tf 11 TL 54 740 Td
(# 42 Bus error recovery) '
() '
(## 42.1 Stuck bus) '
() '
(If the host is reset in the middle of a read, the device may hold SDA low) '
(while it waits for clock pulses to finish a byte. To recover:) '
() '
(  1. Configure SCL as a GPIO output and SDA as an input.) '
(  2. Toggle SCL up to 9 times until SDA reads high.) '
(  3. Generate a STOP condition.) '
(  4. Return the pins to the I\262C peripheral.) '
() '
(## 42.2 NACK on the address byte) '
() '
(A NACK on the address byte means the device is not responding. Causes:) '
() '
(  - The device is in reset \(power-on, brown-out, watchdog, soft reset\). It) '
(    responds again 1.5 ms after the reset \(page 11\).) '
(  - The ADDR pin changed state before the last reset \(page 13\).) '
(  - TEST is not tied to GND \(page 9\).) '
() '
(After the device responds again, read STATUS. If RST = 1, rewrite CONF2.) '
() '
(## 42.3 Wrong data) '
() '
(If CHIP_ID does not read 0x14, the bus is corrupted or a different device) '
(answers. Check pull-ups and cable length \(page 17\).) '
() '
(## 42.4 A reset during a transaction) '
() '
(A reset that occurs in the middle of a transaction aborts it. The host sees) '
(a NACK or a byte of 0xFF. The next successful read of STATUS shows RST = 1.) '
() '
(## 42.5 Recovery is not configuration) '
() '
(Bus recovery restores communication. It does not restore CONF2. After any) '
(recovery, read STATUS and rewrite CONF2 if RST = 1 \(page 45\).) '
ET
BT /F1 7.5 Tf 200.25 36 Td (ENCX14-RM rev 1.2    CC-BY-4.0    page 42 of 60) Tj ET
endstream
endobj
89 0 obj
<< /Type /Page /Parent 2 0 R /MediaBox [0 0 612 792] /Resources << /Font << /F1 3 0 R >> >> /Contents 90 0 R >>
endobj
90 0 obj
<< /Length 1587 >>
stream
BT /F1 9 Tf 11 TL 54 740 Td
(# 43 Power-up sequence) '
() '
(## 43.1 Sequence) '
() '
(  t = 0         VDD rises through VPOR \(2.8 V\). Power-on reset.) '
(  t = 1.5 ms    I\262C interface ready. STATUS = 0x01 \(RST\) plus field flags.) '
(  t = 2.0 ms    First valid angle. MD = 1 if a magnet is present.) '
(  t = 12 ms     AGC settled \(10 ms time constant\).) '
() '
(## 43.2 What the host should do) '
() '
(  1. Wait at least 2 ms after VDD is stable.) '
(  2. Read CHIP_ID \(expect 0x14\) and REV.) '
(  3. Read STATUS. Expect RST = 1 on the first read after power-up.) '
(  4. Write CONF2 with the required FILT, DIR, HYST and WD values.) '
(  5. Read CONF2 back and compare.) '
(  6. Wait 12 ms for the AGC to settle before closing the control loop.) '
(  7. Start the control loop with the per-cycle burst read \(page 40\).) '
() '
(## 43.3 Slow supply ramps) '
() '
(If VDD rises slowly through VPOR and then dips \(for example when a motor) '
(driver on the same rail is enabled\), the device can see a power-on reset) '
(followed by a brown-out reset. The second reset clears the CONF2 value the) '
(host has just written. This is why step 7 must include the STATUS.RST check) '
(on every cycle, not only at start-up.) '
() '
(## 43.4 Host reset without encoder reset) '
() '
(If the host resets but the encoder does not, STATUS.RST is 0 on the first) '
(read and CONF2 still holds the old value. The host should write CONF2) '
(anyway at start-up so that its configuration is known.) '
ET
BT /F1 7.5 Tf 200.25 36 Td (ENCX14-RM rev 1.2    CC-BY-4.0    page 43 of 60) Tj ET
endstream
endobj
91 0 obj
<< /Type /Page /Parent 2 0 R /MediaBox [0 0 612 792] /Resources << /Font << /F1 3 0 R >> >> /Contents 92 0 R >>
endobj
92 0 obj
<< /Length 1621 >>
stream
BT /F1 9 Tf 11 TL 54 740 Td
(# 44 Default register values) '
() '
(Table 44-1. Register values after any reset.) '
() '
(  Addr   Name       Value after reset         Source) '
(  0x00   CHIP_ID    0x14                      fixed) '
(  0x01   REV        0xA1 or 0xA2              fixed) '
(  0x02   CONF1      OTP value \(0x00 if blank\) OTP shadow) '
(  0x03   ZPOS_H     OTP value \(0x00 if blank\) OTP shadow) '
(  0x04   ZPOS_L     OTP value \(0x00 if blank\) OTP shadow) '
(  0x05   MPOS_H     OTP value \(0x3F if blank\) OTP shadow) '
(  0x06   MPOS_L     OTP value \(0xFF if blank\) OTP shadow) '
(  0x08   CONF2      0x00                      volatile) '
(  0x0B   STATUS     0x01 plus field flags     volatile) '
() '
(## 44.1 CONF2 = 0x00 means) '
() '
(  FILT = 0   the noise filter is off; output noise 12 LSB RMS \(table 31-2\)) '
(  DIR = 0    clockwise rotation increases the angle) '
(  HYST = 0   no hysteresis) '
(  WD = 0     watchdog off) '
() '
(## 44.2 "Reset" means every reset) '
() '
(The values in table 44-1 apply after a power-on reset, a brown-out reset, a) '
(watchdog reset and a soft reset. There is no reset source that preserves) '
(CONF2. Revision 1.0 of this manual implied that brown-out resets preserve) '
(CONF2. That was wrong; see errata E-3 on page 58.) '
() '
(## 44.3 Practical consequence) '
() '
(Every configuration value that the application needs and that is not in OTP) '
(must be written by the host after every reset. The host learns about resets) '
(from STATUS.RST \(page 33\).) '
ET
BT /F1 7.5 Tf 200.25 36 Td (ENCX14-RM rev 1.2    CC-BY-4.0    page 44 of 60) Tj ET
endstream
endobj
