94 0 obj
<< /Length 1918 >>
stream
BT /F1 9 Tf 11 TL 54 740 Td
(# 45 Recommended initialisation) '
() '
(## 45.1 Start-up) '
() '
(    def encoder_init\(\):) '
(        wait_ms\(2\)) '
(        assert read\(0x00\) == 0x14            # CHIP_ID) '
(        read\(0x0B\)                           # clear RST from power-up) '
(        write\(0x08, CONF2_VALUE\)             # e.g. 0x03 for FILT = 3) '
(        assert read\(0x08\) == CONF2_VALUE) '
() '
(## 45.2 Every control cycle: rewrite CONF2 after a reset) '
() '
(    def control_step\(\):) '
(        status, angle = burst_read\(0x0B, 3\)  # page 40) '
(        if status & STATUS_RST:              # bit 0, sticky, cleared by read) '
(            write\(0x08, CONF2_VALUE\)         # the reset cleared CONF2) '
(            log_event\("encoder reset"\)) '
(        ...) '
() '
(The rewrite costs one 3-byte write transaction, about 70 \265s at 400 kHz.) '
(Because RST is cleared by the read that reports it, the rewrite must happen) '
(in the same control step. A host that only checks RST at start-up will run) '
(with FILT = 0 after the first brown-out.) '
() '
(## 45.3 Value to write) '
() '
(Choose FILT using page 49. For a robot joint with a 1 kHz loop and a) '
(crossover between 3 and 10 Hz, FILT = 3 is a good starting point) '
(\(CONF2 = 0x03\). Handle direction in host software if possible, so that a) '
(missed rewrite cannot reverse the feedback sign.) '
() '
(## 45.4 Firmware review checklist) '
() '
(  [ ] CONF2 is written at start-up, after CHIP_ID is confirmed.) '
(  [ ] STATUS is read on every control cycle \(burst read from 0x0B\).) '
(  [ ] CONF2 is rewritten in the same cycle whenever STATUS.RST = 1.) '
(  [ ] Encoder resets are counted and logged.) '
(  [ ] The angle is not used while MAGL or MAGH is set or MD is clear.) '
(  [ ] No burst read runs past 0x0F \(errata E-4\).) '
ET
BT /F1 7.5 Tf 200.25 36 Td (ENCX14-RM rev 1.2    CC-BY-4.0    page 45 of 60) Tj ET
endstream
endobj
95 0 obj
<< /Type /Page /Parent 2 0 R /MediaBox [0 0 612 792] /Resources << /Font << /F1 3 0 R >> >> /Contents 96 0 R >>
endobj
96 0 obj
<< /Length 1849 >>
stream
BT /F1 9 Tf 11 TL 54 740 Td
(# 46 OTP programming in production) '
() '
(## 46.1 What OTP stores) '
() '
(OTP holds CONF1, ZPOS and MPOS \(page 29, page 30\). After every reset these) '
(registers are reloaded from OTP. CONF2 is not stored in OTP \(page 36\).) '
() '
(## 46.2 Production procedure) '
() '
(  1. Mount the magnet and complete mechanical assembly.) '
(  2. Supply VDD between 3.2 V and 3.6 V \(errata E-1, page 57\). On rev 0xA2) '
(     silicon 3.0 V is sufficient, but 3.3 V is recommended for all parts.) '
(  3. Read CHIP_ID \(0x14\) and REV. Record REV in the production log.) '
(  4. Move the joint to its mechanical zero and read RAW \(0x0E-0x0F\).) '
(  5. Write ZPOS = RAW. If the joint travels less than a full turn, move to) '
(     the end stop, read RAW and write MPOS.) '
(  6. Write CONF1 if a non-default power mode or ADCSLOW is required.) '
(  7. Write 0x80 to CMD \(0x30\). Wait at least 10 ms without bus access.) '
(  8. Verify the burn as described on page 36.) '
() '
(## 46.3 When not to program OTP) '
() '
(Many robot designs keep ZPOS and MPOS at their defaults and apply the zero) '
(offset in host firmware. This avoids a production step that can be done) '
(only once and lets a joint be re-zeroed after repair. The configuration) '
(that matters for servo loops, CONF2, cannot be programmed in OTP in any) '
(case and must be written by the host after every reset.) '
() '
(## 46.4 Failure modes) '
() '
(  - Burn at VDD below 3.2 V on rev 0xA1: partial burn, ZPOS and MPOS read) '
(    back with random bits \(errata E-1\).) '
(  - Bus access during the 10 ms burn: the burn completes, but the access is) '
(    NACKed and must be repeated.) '
(  - A second burn command is ignored. There is no way to erase OTP.) '
ET
BT /F1 7.5 Tf 200.25 36 Td (ENCX14-RM rev 1.2    CC-BY-4.0    page 46 of 60) Tj ET
endstream
endobj
97 0 obj
<< /Type /Page /Parent 2 0 R /MediaBox [0 0 612 792] /Resources << /Font << /F1 3 0 R >> >> /Contents 98 0 R >>
endobj
98 0 obj
<< /Length 1654 >>
stream
BT /F1 9 Tf 11 TL 54 740 Td
(# 47 Application: servo loops) '
() '
(## 47.1 Typical joint loop) '
() '
(A typical robot joint runs a cascaded loop on a microcontroller:) '
() '
(  - Position loop: 1 kHz, input = encoder angle, output = velocity command.) '
(  - Velocity loop: 1 kHz, input = velocity estimate from the encoder angle,) '
(    output = current command.) '
(  - Current loop: 10 kHz to 20 kHz in the motor driver.) '
() '
(The EncoderX-14 provides the angle for the outer two loops. The host reads) '
(the angle once per control period with a 3-byte burst read from 0x0B) '
(\(page 40\), which also returns STATUS.) '
() '
(## 47.2 What the encoder adds to the loop) '
() '
(  - Delay: the read latency of 2 ms \(page 12\) for a host that polls once) '
(    per 1 ms period and uses the result in the following period.) '
(  - Filter lag: the FILT setting adds the lag of a first-order filter with) '
(    time constant tau = 2^n / 8000 s \(page 31\).) '
(  - Noise: 12 LSB RMS at FILT = 0, less with FILT > 0 \(table 31-2\).) '
(  - Quantisation: 1 LSB = 360 / 16384 = 0.022\260.) '
() '
(## 47.3 Loop design summary) '
() '
(For a joint with a crossover between 3 Hz and 10 Hz:) '
() '
(  - Delay and filter lag together must stay small compared with the phase) '
(    margin. Table 12-1 gives the total lag at 4 Hz.) '
(  - Noise must be small enough that the derivative path does not drive the) '
(    motor with noise \(page 48\).) '
(  - FILT = 3 balances these two needs \(page 49\).) '
() '
(The remaining pages of this part give the details.) '
ET
BT /F1 7.5 Tf 200.25 36 Td (ENCX14-RM rev 1.2    CC-BY-4.0    page 47 of 60) Tj ET
endstream
endobj
99 0 obj
<< /Type /Page /Parent 2 0 R /MediaBox [0 0 612 792] /Resources << /Font << /F1 3 0 R >> >> /Contents 100 0 R >>
endobj
100 0 obj
<< /Length 1946 >>
stream
BT /F1 9 Tf 11 TL 54 740 Td
(# 48 Quantisation and noise in a derivative term) '
() '
(## 48.1 Why the derivative path matters) '
() '
(A PD or PID position loop, and every velocity loop, uses the difference of) '
(two successive angle samples. Differencing multiplies white noise by) '
(sqrt\(2\) and divides it by the sample period. At a 1 kHz loop rate, small) '
(angle noise becomes large velocity noise.) '
() '
(## 48.2 Velocity noise versus FILT \(1 kHz loop\)) '
() '
(Table 48-1. Noise of the one-step difference \(angle[k] - angle[k-1]\) / 1 ms.) '
() '
(  FILT n   Angle noise      Velocity noise      Velocity noise) '
(           \(LSB RMS\)        \(LSB per ms RMS\)    \(\260 / s RMS\)) '
(  0        12.0             17.0                373) '
(  1        6.93             9.8                 215) '
(  2        4.54             6.4                 141) '
(  3        3.10             3.6                 78) '
(  5        1.51             0.8                 17) '
(  7        0.75             0.2                 4) '
() '
(For FILT >= 3 successive 1 ms samples are correlated by the filter, so the) '
(velocity noise falls faster than the angle noise.) '
() '
(## 48.3 Consequences at FILT = 0) '
() '
(  - A derivative gain that is reasonable for the mechanics multiplies) '
(    373 \260/s RMS of noise into the current command.) '
(  - The motor current carries broadband noise. The motor heats, the joint) '
(    buzzes audibly, and a light link can resonate.) '
(  - The error appears as an increase in tracking error and in RMS motor) '
(    current, not as a fault flag. Nothing in STATUS reports it.) '
() '
(## 48.4 Quantisation) '
() '
(At 16384 counts per turn and a 1 kHz loop, one LSB per sample corresponds) '
(to 22 \260/s. Quantisation is small compared with the noise at FILT <= 3 and) '
(can be ignored for loop design.) '
ET
BT /F1 7.5 Tf 200.25 36 Td (ENCX14-RM rev 1.2    CC-BY-4.0    page 48 of 60) Tj ET
endstream
endobj
101 0 obj
<< /Type /Page /Parent 2 0 R /MediaBox [0 0 612 792] /Resources << /Font << /F1 3 0 R >> >> /Contents 102 0 R >>
endobj
102 0 obj
<< /Length 1709 >>
stream
BT /F1 9 Tf 11 TL 54 740 Td
(# 49 Choosing FILT for a servo loop) '
() '
(## 49.1 The trade-off) '
() '
(A larger FILT lowers the noise \(table 31-2, table 48-1\) and adds phase lag) '
(\(table 12-1\). The right value depends on the loop crossover frequency.) '
() '
(## 49.2 Phase lag at the crossover) '
() '
(Table 49-1. Total encoder phase lag \(2 ms read delay plus FILT lag\).) '
() '
(  FILT n   tau       Lag at 4 Hz   Lag at 10 Hz) '
(  0        off       2.9\260          7.2\260) '
(  3        1 ms      4.3\260          10.8\260) '
(  5        4 ms      8.6\260          21.3\260) '
(  7        16 ms     24.8\260         52.4\260) '
() '
(The values at 4 Hz match table 12-1.) '
() '
(## 49.3 Recommendation for robot joints) '
() '
(For a joint with a 1 kHz loop and a crossover between 3 Hz and 10 Hz:) '
() '
(  - FILT = 3 \(CONF2 = 0x03\) is the recommended starting point. It reduces) '
(    the angle noise by a factor of 3.9 and the velocity noise by a factor) '
(    of almost 5, and adds about 1.4\260 of lag at 4 Hz.) '
(  - FILT = 0 is not recommended. The loop is stable, but the derivative) '
(    path drives the motor with noise \(page 48\).) '
(  - FILT >= 5 is only for slow loops. FILT = 7 costs about 25\260 at 4 Hz and) '
(    can make a loop that was stable with FILT = 0 oscillate.) '
() '
(## 49.4 FILT must be maintained) '
() '
(The FILT setting chosen here is lost on every reset, including brown-out) '
(resets that the host does not otherwise see \(page 11, page 58\). Choosing) '
(FILT is half of the job; the other half is restoring it whenever) '
(STATUS.RST = 1 \(page 45\).) '
ET
BT /F1 7.5 Tf 200.25 36 Td (ENCX14-RM rev 1.2    CC-BY-4.0    page 49 of 60) Tj ET
endstream
endobj
103 0 obj
<< /Type /Page /Parent 2 0 R /MediaBox [0 0 612 792] /Resources << /Font << /F1 3 0 R >> >> /Contents 104 0 R >>
endobj
104 0 obj
<< /Length 1661 >>
stream
BT /F1 9 Tf 11 TL 54 740 Td
(# 50 Velocity estimation) '
() '
(## 50.1 No velocity output) '
() '
(The device has no velocity register. The host estimates velocity from) '
(successive angle samples.) '
() '
(## 50.2 Unwrapping) '
() '
(The angle wraps from 16383 to 0. Before differencing, unwrap:) '
() '
(    d = angle[k] - angle[k-1]) '
(    if d > 8192:   d -= 16384) '
(    if d < -8192:  d += 16384) '
(    velocity = d x \(2 x pi / 16384\) / T      # rad/s, T = loop period) '
() '
(This is valid while the joint turns less than half a revolution per loop) '
(period \(30000 rpm at 1 kHz\).) '
() '
(## 50.3 Estimators) '
() '
(  Estimator              Noise          Lag         Note) '
(  One-step difference    highest        T / 2       Use with FILT >= 3) '
(  N-step difference      / N            N x T / 2   Simple, good at low speed) '
(  Tracking observer      tunable        tunable     Best, needs tuning) '
() '
(## 50.4 Interaction with FILT) '
() '
(FILT filters the angle before the host sees it, so it also filters every) '
(velocity estimator that uses the angle. If CONF2 is cleared by a reset,) '
(FILT drops to 0 and the velocity noise rises by a factor of about 5 at) '
(1 kHz \(table 48-1\) without any change in host code. The velocity estimate) '
(is often the first signal where a lost CONF2 becomes visible in logs.) '
() '
(## 50.5 Direction) '
() '
(If the host relies on DIR = 1 \(page 32\), a reset returns DIR to 0 and the) '
(sign of the velocity estimate reverses. Handle direction in host software) '
(where possible \(page 15\).) '
ET
BT /F1 7.5 Tf 200.25 36 Td (ENCX14-RM rev 1.2    CC-BY-4.0    page 50 of 60) Tj ET
endstream
endobj
105 0 obj
<< /Type /Page /Parent 2 0 R /MediaBox [0 0 612 792] /Resources << /Font << /F1 3 0 R >> >> /Contents 106 0 R >>
endobj
106 0 obj
<< /Length 1805 >>
stream
BT /F1 9 Tf 11 TL 54 740 Td
(# 51 Diagnostics overview) '
() '
(## 51.1 What the device reports) '
() '
(  Signal        Where            Meaning                          Page) '
(  RST           STATUS bit 0     A reset occurred \(sticky\)        33, 53) '
(  MAGL          STATUS bit 1     Field too weak                   52) '
(  MAGH          STATUS bit 2     Field too strong                 52) '
(  MD            STATUS bit 3     Magnet detected                  52) '
(  OVF           STATUS bit 4     CORDIC overflow \(sticky\)         52) '
(  AGC           0x1A             Front-end gain                   35, 52) '
(  MAG           0x1B-0x1C        Field magnitude                  35, 52) '
(  NACK          I\262C              Bus or device fault              54) '
() '
(## 51.2 Reset sources and their evidence) '
() '
(All four reset sources \(power-on, brown-out, watchdog, soft reset, page 11\)) '
(have the same effect and the same evidence: STATUS.RST = 1 on the next) '
(STATUS read. The device does not record which source caused the reset.) '
(A host can narrow it down:) '
() '
(  - Soft reset: the host itself wrote 0xA5 to CMD.) '
(  - Power-on: the host also restarted, or the encoder supply was switched.) '
(  - Watchdog: only possible with CONF2.WD = 1 \(page 54\).) '
(  - Brown-out: everything else. In a running joint this is the most common) '
(    cause \(page 53\).) '
() '
(## 51.3 What the device does not report) '
() '
(  - It does not report that CONF2 has changed. The only evidence is RST.) '
(  - It does not report excessive noise. Noise is a property of the FILT) '
(    setting and the field, not a fault.) '
(  - It does not report a stalled host or a missed read.) '
ET
BT /F1 7.5 Tf 200.25 36 Td (ENCX14-RM rev 1.2    CC-BY-4.0    page 51 of 60) Tj ET
endstream
endobj
107 0 obj
<< /Type /Page /Parent 2 0 R /MediaBox [0 0 612 792] /Resources << /Font << /F1 3 0 R >> >> /Contents 108 0 R >>
endobj
108 0 obj
<< /Length 1701 >>
stream
BT /F1 9 Tf 11 TL 54 740 Td
(# 52 Magnet and field faults) '
() '
(## 52.1 Field flags) '
() '
(  Condition                   Flags                  Angle valid) '
(  Field 30 mT to 90 mT        MD = 1                 yes) '
(  Field below 30 mT           MAGL = 1, MD = 1       no) '
(  Field above 90 mT           MAGH = 1, MD = 1       no) '
(  Field below 20 mT           MAGL = 1, MD = 0       no) '
(  Field above 120 mT          MAGH = 1, MD = 0       no) '
() '
(MAGL, MAGH and MD are not sticky; they reflect the field at the time of) '
(the read. OVF is sticky and cleared by a STATUS read.) '
() '
(## 52.2 Typical causes) '
() '
(  - Air gap too large \(field too weak\): see the air gap table on page 20.) '
(  - Air gap too small or wrong magnet grade \(field too strong\).) '
(  - Magnet loosened or cracked: MAG changes over time, AGC drifts.) '
(  - External field from a nearby motor: AGC and MAG change with motor) '
(    current.) '
() '
(## 52.3 Host response) '
() '
(When the angle is not valid, hold the last motor command or bring the) '
(joint to a safe state. Do not use the angle \(page 40\). Log MAG and AGC) '
(with the event.) '
() '
(## 52.4 Trend monitoring) '
() '
(AGC moves slowly as the magnet ages and with temperature \(page 35\). A) '
(change of more than 20 % from the value recorded at production test) '
(indicates a mechanical problem.) '
() '
(## 52.5 Power-up glitch on rev 0xA1) '
() '
(On rev 0xA1 silicon MAGH can be set for up to 1 ms after a power-on reset) '
(even with a correct field \(errata E-2, page 57\). Wait 2 ms after reset) '
(before acting on MAGH.) '
ET
BT /F1 7.5 Tf 200.25 36 Td (ENCX14-RM rev 1.2    CC-BY-4.0    page 52 of 60) Tj ET
endstream
endobj
109 0 obj
<< /Type /Page /Parent 2 0 R /MediaBox [0 0 612 792] /Resources << /Font << /F1 3 0 R >> >> /Contents 110 0 R >>
endobj
110 0 obj
<< /Length 2229 >>
stream
BT /F1 9 Tf 11 TL 54 740 Td
(# 53 Brown-out events in the field) '
() '
(## 53.1 How they happen) '
() '
(In a robot joint the encoder often shares the 3.3 V rail with the logic) '
(that drives the motor. A step in motor current can pull the rail below) '
(VBOR = 2.6 V for a few microseconds. A dip longer than the brown-out filter) '
(time tBOR \(typ. 2 \265s, page 11\) resets the device.) '
() '
(## 53.2 What the host sees) '
() '
(  - No bus error. The I\262C interface is ready 1.5 ms after the reset; a read) '
(    that falls inside that window is NACKed, later reads succeed.) '
(  - The angle keeps arriving. It is valid again 2 ms after the reset.) '
(  - STATUS.RST = 1 on the next STATUS read, then 0 again.) '
(  - CONF2 = 0x00: FILT = 0, DIR = 0, HYST = 0, WD = 0.) '
() '
(The joint keeps moving, but with the filter off. Velocity noise rises by a) '
(factor of about 5 \(table 48-1\). Tracking error and motor current rise, the) '
(joint may buzz. If the host relied on DIR = 1, the feedback sign reverses.) '
() '
(## 53.3 Detecting a brown-out) '
() '
(The only reliable evidence is STATUS.RST. Read STATUS on every control) '
(cycle with the 3-byte burst read from 0x0B \(page 40\) and test bit 0. A) '
(host that reads STATUS only at start-up will not detect a brown-out.) '
() '
(Indirect evidence, useful when analysing logs after the fact:) '
() '
(  - A step increase in angle noise or velocity noise with no mechanical) '
(    change.) '
(  - A single NACK on the encoder address during a motor current step.) '
(  - A coincidence between motor current steps and the change in noise.) '
() '
(## 53.4 Required response) '
() '
(In the same control step in which RST = 1 is read, rewrite CONF2 \(page 45,) '
(errata E-3 on page 58\). Count and log the event. If the count rises over) '
(time, improve supply decoupling \(page 17\); do not rely on decoupling alone.) '
() '
(## 53.5 Frequency) '
() '
(Field data from robot joints with shared supplies shows brown-out rates) '
(from less than one per year to several per hour, depending on layout and) '
(motor current. A design must assume that brown-outs happen.) '
ET
BT /F1 7.5 Tf 200.25 36 Td (ENCX14-RM rev 1.2    CC-BY-4.0    page 53 of 60) Tj ET
endstream
endobj
111 0 obj
<< /Type /Page /Parent 2 0 R /MediaBox [0 0 612 792] /Resources << /Font << /F1 3 0 R >> >> /Contents 112 0 R >>
endobj
112 0 obj
<< /Length 1463 >>
stream
BT /F1 9 Tf 11 TL 54 740 Td
(# 54 Watchdog and bus faults) '
() '
(## 54.1 Watchdog) '
() '
(With CONF2.WD = 1 the device resets itself if the internal oscillator) '
(stalls for more than 1 ms. The watchdog reset has the same effect as every) '
(other reset: CONF2 = 0x00 and STATUS.RST = 1. Because WD lives in CONF2,) '
(the watchdog is disabled again after the reset until the host rewrites) '
(CONF2.) '
() '
(## 54.2 Bus faults) '
() '
(  Symptom                    Likely cause                     Action) '
(  NACK on address            device in reset or not powered   retry next cycle) '
(  NACK on data               soft reset write \(expected\)      none) '
(  SDA held low               interrupted transaction          bus recovery) '
(  Wrong data after 0x0F      burst ran past 0x0F \(E-4\)        limit burst) '
(  Repeated NACK              wiring, ADDR pin, supply         stop, report) '
() '
(Bus recovery is described on page 42.) '
() '
(## 54.3 Host-side timeout) '
() '
(The host should treat more than three consecutive failed reads as an) '
(encoder fault and bring the joint to a safe state. A single NACK is normal) '
(after a reset and must not trip the joint.) '
() '
(## 54.4 After a bus fault) '
() '
(After any bus fault that lasted longer than 1.5 ms, read STATUS and check) '
(RST before using the angle. The fault may have been a reset.) '
ET
BT /F1 7.5 Tf 200.25 36 Td (ENCX14-RM rev 1.2    CC-BY-4.0    page 54 of 60) Tj ET
endstream
endobj
113 0 obj
<< /Type /Page /Parent 2 0 R /MediaBox [0 0 612 792] /Resources << /Font << /F1 3 0 R >> >> /Contents 114 0 R >>
endobj
114 0 obj
<< /Length 1195 >>
stream
BT /F1 9 Tf 11 TL 54 740 Td
(# 55 Reliability data) '
() '
(## 55.1 Failure rate) '
() '
(Table 55-1. Reliability \(JESD85 method, 60 % confidence\).) '
() '
(  Parameter                     Condition           Value     Unit) '
(  FIT                           55 \260C junction      4.1       per 10^9 h) '
(  MTTF                          55 \260C junction      2.8e7     h) '
(  Early life failure rate                           < 50      ppm) '
() '
(## 55.2 OTP data retention) '
() '
(  Condition              Retention) '
(  125 \260C                 10 years) '
(  85 \260C                  > 50 years) '
() '
(## 55.3 Magnet ageing) '
() '
(The device itself does not age measurably. NdFeB magnets lose about 0.1 %) '
(of remanence per 1000 h at 100 \260C. Over a product life this changes the) '
(field by a few percent, which the AGC compensates \(page 35, page 52\).) '
() '
(## 55.4 Reliability and resets) '
() '
(Brown-out resets do not damage the device and do not count against its) '
(reliability. They affect the application through the loss of CONF2) '
(\(page 58\), not through wear.) '
ET
BT /F1 7.5 Tf 200.25 36 Td (ENCX14-RM rev 1.2    CC-BY-4.0    page 55 of 60) Tj ET
endstream
endobj
115 0 obj
<< /Type /Page /Parent 2 0 R /MediaBox [0 0 612 792] /Resources << /Font << /F1 3 0 R >> >> /Contents 116 0 R >>
endobj
116 0 obj
<< /Length 1512 >>
stream
BT /F1 9 Tf 11 TL 54 740 Td
(# 56 Qualification and ESD) '
() '
(## 56.1 Qualification summary) '
() '
(Table 56-1. Qualification tests.) '
() '
(  Test                        Standard       Condition                Result) '
(  HTOL                        JESD22-A108    125 \260C, 3.6 V, 1000 h    0 / 231) '
(  Temperature cycling         JESD22-A104    -55 \260C to 150 \260C, 1000x  0 / 231) '
(  HAST                        JESD22-A110    130 \260C, 85 % RH, 96 h    0 / 231) '
(  High-temperature storage    JESD22-A103    150 \260C, 1000 h           0 / 77) '
(  Latch-up                    JESD78         \261100 mA, 85 \260C           pass) '
(  MSL                         J-STD-020      260 \260C reflow            MSL 3) '
() '
(## 56.2 ESD ratings) '
() '
(  Model                       Standard       Rating) '
(  Human body model \(HBM\)      JS-001         \2612 kV) '
(  Charged device model \(CDM\)  JS-002         \261500 V) '
() '
(## 56.3 Handling) '
() '
(Handle the device at an ESD-protected workstation \(page 4\). The SDA, SCL) '
(and ADDR pins are not protected against system-level ESD; add external) '
(protection if the I\262C bus leaves the board.) '
() '
(## 56.4 Supply transients) '
() '
(The device tolerates supply transients within the absolute maximum ratings) '
(\(page 9\). Transients that pull VDD below VBOR cause a brown-out reset) '
(\(page 11\); they do not damage the device.) '
ET
BT /F1 7.5 Tf 200.25 36 Td (ENCX14-RM rev 1.2    CC-BY-4.0    page 56 of 60) Tj ET
endstream
endobj
